- yash-suite-other-hangs: `tests/yash/random-y.tst` (formerly
  `yash-random-y-tst-hangs`) no longer hangs -- confirmed fixed as a
  side effect of unrelated 2026-07-30 work (most likely
  `fixes/111`/`fixes/112`, both about pipeline/redirection-failure
  control flow); it now completes in well under a second and, once
  actually inspected, turned up three genuine `$RANDOM` bugs (seeding,
  non-integer assignment, and `unset` not disabling the magic), fixed
  separately as `fixes/113`.

  However, a full sweep of all 119 `tests/yash/*.tst` files (each run
  individually with a 10s timeout via `run-tst.sh`, same as CTest's own
  per-file `TIMEOUT` but much shorter to survey quickly) found several
  *other* files that still hang or run suspiciously long, so
  `DO_YASH_TESTS` staying off by default (see `TODO.md`) is still
  correct, just for a different, not-yet-isolated reason:

    arith-y.tst, cmdprint-y.tst, pipeline-y.tst, redir-y.tst,
    until-y.tst, while-y.tst -- all hit the 10s timeout (still running,
    not just slow).

    function-y.tst (~8s), if-y.tst (~9.5s), typeset-y.tst (~8.5s) --
    finished but suspiciously slow for a conformance test file; may or
    may not be a real bug (could just be an intentionally slow test
    case), not investigated.

  None of these are isolated to a specific line/case yet -- same next
  step as before applies (debug build + debugger, or bisecting each
  file's test cases individually via a copy with cases removed) but
  hasn't been done for any of them.

- eval-lineno-imprecise-inside-function: `$LINENO` read from within an
  `eval`'d string now counts from the invoking statement's line
  (fixed, see fixes/92) rather than always reporting 1, but the value
  it inherits (`parse_lineno`, stamped once per top-level list by
  `sh_loop()`) is the *call site's* line, not the eval statement's own
  line when eval runs from inside a function or loop body whose
  definition spans multiple lines -- there is no per-command location
  tracking to fall back on for that case (unlike a direct, non-eval'd
  `$LINENO` reference, which reads the parse-time `loc` stamped
  individually on its own `N_ARGPARAM` node). Confirmed:

    f() {
      echo $LINENO   # correctly prints 2
      eval 'echo $LINENO'   # prints 5 (f's call site), should print 3
    }
    f

- set-notify-unimplemented: POSIX `-b` (report a background job's
  completion as soon as it happens, instead of waiting until just
  before the next prompt) is missing the same way -- no `struct
  shopt` bit, not recognized by `builtin_set()`. Belongs in `src/job/`:
  `job_wait.c`/`sh_onsig()`'s SIGCHLD path and `job_clean.c` are where
  a finished background job is currently *noticed*, but
  `job_clean.c`'s own banner-printing comments already describe the
  existing race between the async SIGCHLD path and the synchronous
  `job_wait()` path around exactly this "print immediately vs. defer"
  distinction (a stray banner interrupting a running foreground
  command's own output mid-line is the whole reason it's deferred by
  default) -- so this is more than flipping a condition, it's adding
  a real "OK to interrupt terminal output right now" toggle to logic
  that currently assumes deferral is always correct.

- set-verbose-unimplemented: POSIX `-v` (echo each input line to
  stderr as it's read, before any parsing/expansion touches it --
  distinct from `-x`, which echoes *after* expansion, from
  `src/eval/`'s `E_PRINT` path) is missing the same way. There is
  currently no hook point that sees "a raw input line" as a unit
  separate from `src/source.h`'s character-at-a-time
  `source_peek()`/`source_next()` interface the parser reads through
  -- implementing this needs either a small line-buffering layer at
  that level (echo-and-flush whenever a newline is consumed) or some
  other way to reconstruct "the line just parsed" from `struct
  source`'s position tracking. This is the most structurally invasive
  of this group precisely because nothing else needs "raw source
  text" as such a unit today.

- set-histexpand-unimplemented: `struct shopt`'s `histexpand` bit
  (`src/sh.h`, `-H`) is set correctly by `set -H`/`set +H` and
  reflected back correctly in `$-`, but nothing checks it either --
  more fundamentally, there is no history-expansion ('!') feature
  implemented at all under `src/history/` or `src/term/` for this
  flag to gate. It's a placeholder with nothing behind it.
